Exactly the right instinct — building the receiver demystifies the whole pattern. Here it is, complete:

```js
function forEach(arr, callback) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    callback(arr[i], i);
  }
}

forEach(['tea', 'coffee', 'juice'], (drink, i) => {
  console.log(i, drink);
});
```

That prints `0 tea`, `1 coffee`, `2 juice`. And now the pattern has no mystery left: a callback is just a parameter whose value happens to be a function, and "calling it back" is an ordinary function call that happens to sit inside a loop you wrote.

Notice what building it teaches that using it doesn't. *You* chose the contract: this `forEach` passes the value and the index. The real `Array.prototype.forEach` passes a third argument, the array itself — a design decision its authors made and documented. Every callback API you will ever use is this same five-line idea with a policy attached: when to call, how many times, with what arguments, and what to do if the callback throws. When an API surprises you, those four policy questions are where the surprise lives.

