With a function call — because a function call is the original way to manufacture a fresh variable. The pattern is called an IIFE (immediately invoked function expression):

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function (j) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log('i is', j), 5);
  })(i);
}
```

This prints 0, 1, 2. Walk the mechanism: each loop iteration *calls* the wrapper function, passing the current `i`. A call creates a brand-new parameter `j`, and parameters are per-call — three calls, three independent `j` variables, holding 0, 1, and 2. The callback captures its call's `j`, and since `j` is never touched again, the value survives until the timer fires.

Notice what this reveals: `let` didn't add new power, it automated this. A per-iteration binding is exactly "a fresh variable per pass," and the IIFE achieved it manually with the only scoping tool the old language had — the function call.

A modern variant of the same trick survives even in `let` code: passing the loop variable into a helper that registers the callback (`items.forEach(item => register(item))`) works for the identical reason — each call, each binding.

