Yes, and it's the canonical demonstration that closures provide real privacy. In JavaScript:

```js
function makeAccount(opening) {
  let balance = opening;
  return {
    deposit(n)  { balance += n; return balance; },
    withdraw(n) { if (n > balance) return 'insufficient'; balance -= n; return balance; },
    balance()   { return balance; }
  };
}

const acct = makeAccount(100);
acct.deposit(40);    // 140
acct.withdraw(200);  // 'insufficient'
acct.balance;        // undefined — there is no such property to read
```

The three methods share one captured `balance` variable. There is no property holding it — `acct` has only the three functions — so no outside code can read or corrupt the balance except through the methods, which enforce the rules (no overdraft). This is the same guarantee a class private field (`#balance`) gives you, achieved with scope alone.

The pattern generalizes into what people call the module pattern: a factory returns an object of functions, and everything not returned is private by construction. Its limits are the honest ones: no prototype sharing (each account's methods are fresh copies — fine at this scale), and no easy way to add a method later from outside. When you outgrow those, that's the signal to graduate to a class — not before.

