The call stack is the pile of *unfinished* function calls. Every time a function calls another function, the caller is paused and pushed onto the pile; the callee runs on top. When a function returns, it's popped off and the one beneath resumes. At any instant, only the top of the pile is actually executing.

You can watch the pile grow and shrink with entry/exit logs:

```js
function toast() { console.log('  toast: in'); console.log('  toast: out'); }
function sandwich() {
  console.log(' sandwich: in');
  toast();
  console.log(' sandwich: out');
}
function order() { console.log('order: in'); sandwich(); console.log('order: out'); }
order();
```

```
order: in
 sandwich: in
  toast: in
  toast: out
 sandwich: out
order: out
```

The indentation is the stack depth. `order` can't print its "out" line until `sandwich` returns; `sandwich` waits on `toast`. Last in, first out — a stack in the literal data-structure sense.

Why it matters for callbacks: **an async callback can only start when this pile is completely empty.** When a timer fires, its callback isn't inserted into anyone's pile — it becomes a *new* pile of height one, started fresh by the event loop. That's why an exception in a timer callback can't be caught by a `try/catch` in the code that set the timer: that code's frame left the stack long ago. And it's why "stack overflow" means what it says — a function that calls itself without a base case pushes frames until the pile exceeds its limit, which in recursion puzzles is the pile you just learned to draw.

